  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong to severe thunderstorms today. More showers \nand storms possible Sunday morning. Dry to start the work week.\n\nStorms have reached the area, and have been ongoing since this \nmorning, with the most intense activity having occurred west of\nInterstate 77 in VA, with scattered severe storms in the NC\nFoothills and Piedmont at this time. These clusters of storms \nhave resulted in numerous reports of downed trees, and \nassociated outflows out ahead of the storms have also brought \ndown several trees, not surprising given DCAPE on the sounding \nat RNK already 1100 J/kg as of 12Z this morning. The storms are \nriding along a strong DCAPE gradient, roughly oriented SW to NE \nfrom NC into southern VA. These storms should be out of the \narea by the early to mid afternoon. Some CAMs are indicating \nredevelopment of convection, but more scattered in nature, in \nthe NC Piedmont by later today. These areas have been under \nclear skies, with temperatures already in the 90s. SBCAPE over \nthat region is over 2500 J/kg, and 3000 J/kg or higher along and\nsouth of VA/NC border. DCAPE is forecast to remain high, \nparticularly over central NC, so any storms later today will \nstill have the potential to produce damaging wind gusts. \n\nThe cold front sags south tonight, which could keep some isolated \nshowers and possible storms over the Blue Ridge during the overnight \nhours. By Sunday, the front will be over NC, so most shower and \nstorm activity will come to an end for most of the region. However, \nwith some moisture still in the vicinity of the front, and mid level \nshortwave disturbances moving in, can still expect some isolated \nshowers and storms along and west of the Blue Ridge, especially west \nof Interstate 77, during the afternoon and evening.\n\nA broad surface high pressure system moves across the north eastern \nUS by Monday, and will build southward into the region, prompting a \nfew days of quieter weather. Another front approaches the area late \nin the week, which will bring the potential for more showers and \nstorms, especially as the heat builds once again.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Break in the heat late in the weekend, but heat \nbuilds again towards the middle of next week.\n\nSunday and Monday will be cooler, with high temperatures in the mid \n70s in the west and low to mid 80s in the east, only a couple \ndegrees above normal. The 500mb ridge will move back towards the \neast ridge and will become more centered over the southern and \neastern US, which will bring the return to hot temperatures by the \nmiddle part of next week, though does not look to be quite as hot as \nlast week at this time. A frontal passage by the end of the week \nwill help to break down the ridge, and bring some cooler conditions \nback to the area.",
